 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the grid. Each voxel is processed by one thread (or thread group) in the compute shader [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input voxel grid and $O(M)$ for the output mesh, where $M$ is the number of triangles (bounded by `MaxTriangle`) [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Highly parallelizable on GPU. Performance is limited by memory bandwidth for voxel sampling and atomic contention on the counter buffer [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Deterministic memory usage, GPU-friendly, suitable for real-time or high-throughput applications, simple topology [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed topology (cannot handle arbitrary topology changes like merging/splitting as easily as adaptive methods), requires pre-allocated buffer size, may produce noisy normals if gradients are not smoothed [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When converting a dense, uniform SDF grid to a mesh on the GPU, especially for real-time visualization or when memory predictability is critical [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the grid is sparse or adaptive (use Octree-based Isosurfacing), or when sharp features need to be preserved without grid alignment (use Extended Marching Cubes) [S1, S3].
